{
  "q0": "Project Manager thought choosing a stylish fruit for remote design would be good, specifically mentioning banana as a good option that fits the company color scheme and is ergonomic. They agreed it was 'really a good point' when User Interface mentioned the banana connection to company colors.",
  "q1": "Marketing thought the remote should have a 'fancy look and feel' with fruits and vegetables as the theme, specifically suggesting a pear as an option that's easy to hold and mentioned they thought a pear would be good for the design.",
  "q2": "The discussion about choosing a stylish fruit for remote design involved considering banana and pear as options, focusing on ergonomics, spongy materials for robustness, color considerations, and practicality for both right and left hand users. They agreed banana would be a good option due to its ergonomic shape, color, and connection to the company's color scheme.",
  "q3": "User Interface thought the remote should have channel numbers placed 'somewhere a bit out of the way' and suggested adding a vibrator inside the remote. They also mentioned the idea of a remote that could be thrown (with a sensor to detect when it hits the television)."
}

{
  "q0": "The project requirements were amended to focus on designing a remote specifically for elderly users (60+), keep teletext for subtitles, implement minimal buttons (2-3), add a small LCD screen for button explanations, and consider a docking station to address remote loss. They decided to use simple plastics rather than high-tech materials to keep costs low.",
  "q1": "The discussion concluded that the remote should have minimal buttons (2-3) with basic functions (channel selection, volume control, power, and teletext), no numeric keypad or scroll/joystick features, and a small LCD screen showing button functions rather than a touchscreen.",
  "q2": "The docking station was considered to address the problem that 50% of users indicated their remotes tend to get lost in their room. It would function by sending a signal when docked to make the remote beep, indicating its location, and potentially recharge batteries. They decided it could be implemented if the remote stays small and not too fancy.",
  "q3": "Marketing disagreed with the Project Manager's focus on a 'fancy design' because they argued that for a TV remote (which has minimal functions), there's not much room for innovation, and elderly users would accept a standard remote rather than an innovative design that would be difficult to compete with existing products."
}
